rmed since 1986.","rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 57\u00b0F was typical for November 13.","t_yest":"November 13 was a typical day","p_yest":"The high of <strong>57\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 61\u00b0F, and the record is 80\u00b0F (1912, 1915). The low of <strong>41\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Greensboro has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Greensboro's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Greensboro has set <strong class=\"sig\">18 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 79 that chance alone would give, and <strong>69 record highs</strong>, close to the 79 that chance alone would give. The 1910s hold the most record highs of any decade (80). Greensboro's all-time high is 104\u00b0F, on June 12, 1911.","t_chg":"Greensboro's nights have warmed. Its days have warmed since 1986.","p_chg":"Across 121 complete years: Greensboro's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.21\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.14 to +0.28), about +2.6\u00b0F since 1903. Greensboro's average high shows no clear change: \u22120.01\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.08 to +0.06) that includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.78\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.50 to +1.05) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.48\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.13 to +0.83). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 11 of 12 months. Highs rose in none; May and September fell.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Greensboro's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.13 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 43.9 inches a year. The wettest year was 2018 (64.11 in); the driest, 1941 (27.74 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 12 daily record highs, 1 daily record low"}}